cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "Dimensional Asymptotics of Euler-Based Simulated Likelihood for Multidimensional Diffusions"
abstract: >-
  A widely used simulation method for likelihood inference in discretely observed
  diffusions approximates each transition density by simulating an Euler scheme to one
  step before the observed endpoint and averaging the final Gaussian density. This work
  shows that the endpoint estimator is a shrinking-bandwidth kernel estimator whose
  effective simulation size is S/M^(K/2) rather than S, where K is the state dimension,
  M the number of Euler substeps and S the number of simulations. It derives exact
  Brownian moments, the general local moment law, the corrected density-level
  normalisation, and the pointwise mean squared error, minimised at M ~ S^(2/(K+4)). It
  then gives a direct counterexample to the joint transition-density statements of
  Brandt and Santa-Clara (2002): along M = S, a sequence satisfying their own rate
  condition and regularity assumptions, the simulated density converges in probability
  to zero for every K > 2 although the true density is strictly positive. This refutes
  the consistency asserted in their Lemma 2 and the central-limit statement of their
  Lemma 3, so the proofs of their parameter-estimator theorems fail. Inconsistency of
  the resulting argmax estimator is not proved. In a Gaussian location model a
  conditional argument gives the opposite, that the simulated maximiser is consistent
  along the very sequences on which the density collapses, so that the counterexample
  would not transfer; it rests on a uniform-integrability condition for the empirical
  tails which is stated but not verified. The rate is a separate matter. For the
  nearest-atom proxy criterion the score at the truth and the curvature there are both
  obtained exactly, and they predict that the estimator is not root-N consistent, which
  would contradict the conclusion of the published Lemma 5 rather than its proof; the
  step joining them is confirmed numerically but not established, and is set out
  explicitly. Issues specific to the exchange-rate application of that paper,
  which concern its specification rather than the estimator, are treated in a companion
  note deposited in the same record.
authors:
